1715: The conclusion is that the quotient by the torus associated to this natural rank $3$ sub-local
1716: system is a map $\bS^6\to Q$, where $Q$ is a Seifert fibered $3$-manifold over $\bP^1\simeq \bS^2$ 
1717: with a multiple circle of multiplicity $3$ over $0$ and multiplicity $4$ over $1$. The condition 
1718: $|12\psi(a_0+a_1)|=1$ in Theorem \ref{main-thm} translates exactly into the condition
1719: $Q\simeq \bS^3$ with $\bS^3\to \bS^2$ the Seifert fibration with multiplicities $3$ and $4$ over
1720: two points \cite[Ch.~1]{Orl72}. Thus the quotient map is a map $\bS^6\to \bS^3$.
1721: \end{remark}
1722: 
1723: \begin{remark} A very general smooth fiber $F$ of $X(u)\to\bP^1$, or equivalently $Y\to\bP^1$, 
1724: is not an abelian variety, but one can check \cite[\S 9.1]{AC26} that it admits a monodromy-invariant
1725:  Hodge class $$\xi\in H^{1,1}(F)\cap H^2(F,\bZ).$$ The class $\xi$ (see Lemma \ref{invariant-form})
1726:  is thus the first Chern class of a line bundle. Its type, as a symplectic form, is $(1,6)$. On $F^0H_1(F,\bC)$, the
1727:  associated Hodge form $i\xi(v,\bar v)$ has signature $(1,1)$. Thus we have a natural
1728:  period mapping $$\bP^1\setminus \{0,1,\infty\}\to {\rm Sp}(\bZ^4,\xi)\backslash {\rm Sp}_4(\bR)/{\rm U}(1,1).$$
1729:  The difference from the abelian case is that the action of ${\rm Sp}(\bZ^4,\xi)$ on the domain of 
1730:  complex tori $\bD\coloneqq {\rm Sp}_4(\bR)/{\rm U}(1,1)$ is not properly discontinuous. But, after all, the monodromy
1731:  is not all of ${\rm Sp}(\bZ^4,\xi)$---indeed, the monodromy preserves the filtration (\ref{filt1}, \ref{filt2}) and so lies
1732:  in a parabolic subgroup $P_\bZ\subset \Sp(\bZ^4,\xi)$.
1733:  B.~Klingler suggested the possibility that the monodromy group
1734:  could act properly discontinuously on $\bD$, or at least on an analytic open set
1735:  containing the period image.
1736: \end{remark}
1737: 
1738: \begin{remark} The constructions of Sections \ref{sec-initial} and \ref{sec-log-mod} work in significantly greater
1739: generality than this one example. Indeed, consider an elliptic surface $\pi\colon S\to C$ with section, a line bundle
1740: $M\in {\rm Pic}(S)$ which lies in ${\rm Pic}^0$ of the general fiber, and a translation $t\in {\rm Aut}(S/C)$.
1741: Suppose $\cH om(t^*M,M)\simeq \pi^*\cO_C(\pm E)$ for $E$ an effective divisor 
1742: (e.g.~this holds when $C\simeq \bP^1$ and $t$ preserves the components of the Kodaira fibers).
1743: We get an associated linearization $\phi\in {\rm Aut}(M^{\times,\circ})$ of the principal
1744: $\bG_m$-bundle $M^\times$ over the complement of the fibers over $E$.
1745: 
1746: Then $\phi$ has either zeros or poles along the inverse image of $E$, according to the sign.
1747: In the former case, scale $\phi$ by a sufficiently small $u\in\bC^*$; in the latter,
1748: by a sufficiently large one. This action is properly discontinuous by the argument 
1749: of Proposition \ref{discontinuous} (since the linearization 
1750: has only zeroes or only poles, some scaling is uniformly contracting
1751: or expanding). Furthermore, we can fill the quotient by a Mumford construction, 
1752: as in Proposition \ref{mumford-1}. One may then perform log transforms, at some
1753: finite number of fibers.
1754: 
1755: It is unclear what class of $6$-manifolds can arise from such a construction.
1756: This line of inquiry is pursued in {\it Threefold Explorer}, an applet powered
1757: by SageMath. It automates 
1758: van Kampen and Mayer--Vietoris computations of the fundamental group and integral
1759: cohomology of a broad class of such constructions \cite{Eng26}.
1760: \end{remark}
